---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import CrewPlanner from '../../components/CrewPlanner.astro';
import { FAQS } from '../../data/game';
import { GAME_IMAGES } from '../../lib/images';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Co-op guide', path: '/guides/co-op/' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'status', label: 'Current co-op status' },
  { id: 'confirmed', label: 'What is confirmed' },
  { id: 'unknown', label: 'What is not confirmed' },
  { id: 'start', label: 'Starting a session' },
  { id: 'crew-planner', label: 'Crew planner' },
  { id: 'roles', label: 'Role strategy' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'Co-op questions' },
];

const faq = FAQS.coOp;
const block = 'scroll-mt-28';
---

<GuideLayout
  seoTitle="Main Sequence Co-op Guide: Multiplayer Status & Crew Planner"
  description="How Main Sequence co-op works: each player captains a ship, the demo supports multiplayer, and our planner helps split logistics, defense and exploration."
  eyebrow="Multiplayer"
  title="Main Sequence co-op guide and crew planner"
  intro="Co-op is in the demo, but it is still a moving target. Here is what the developer has confirmed, what remains unknown, and a practical way to split the first shift."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  sourceIds={[
    'steam-store',
    'steam-news-2026-10-19',
    'steam-news-2026-09-09',
    'gamesfinity',
    'steam-news-2026-10-21',
    'steam-forum-docks',
  ]}
  image={GAME_IMAGES['main-sequence-fleet-management.jpg']}
  imageAlt="The Main Sequence fleet management interface listing stations and ships in a co-op industrial network"
  imageCaption="Fleet management becomes a shared logistics problem in co-op. Official Steam screenshot."
  meta={['Online & LAN co-op', 'Demo support added Oct 19', 'Player cap unconfirmed', 'Crew planner included']}
  faq={faq}
  pageType="WebPage"
  dataNote="Multiplayer is a focus of ongoing development. Re-test the current demo before planning a large crew; player count and stability may change."
>
  <section id="status" class={block}>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">Current status</span>
      <p class="m-0">
        Steam lists single-player, online co-op and LAN co-op. The October 19,
        2026 Next Fest update added co-op multiplayer to the demo. The developer
        describes each player as the captain of their own ship. No maximum
        player count, dedicated-server support or cross-play details have been
        published.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      The public material presents co-op as the same industrial sandbox, with
      more captains able to operate ships and stations — not a separate
      competitive mode. The developer’s examples split work between logistics,
      defense and exploration, but the game does not appear to enforce classes
      or a single shared role.
    </p>
    <p>
      During the September 2026 playtest, multiplayer was explicitly
      experimental, with performance and stability called an ongoing focus
      before Early Access. The October demo update improved multiplayer, but it
      is still not a finished competitive or dedicated-server product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="confirmed" class={block}>
    <h2>What is confirmed</h2>
    <div class="data-table-wrap not-prose mt-5">
      <table class="data-table data-table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Feature</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
            <th scope="col">Detail</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Feature">Online co-op</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Confirmed</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Listed on the Steam store and added to the demo in the October 19 update.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Feature">LAN co-op</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Confirmed</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Listed as a Steam feature on the main game page.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Feature">Own ship per player</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Confirmed</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">The official store description says every co-op player is the captain of their own ship.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Feature">Flexible division of work</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Confirmed</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Logistics, defense and exploration are given as examples, not locked classes.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="cell-name" data-label="Feature">Demo multiplayer</td>
            <td data-label="Status">Confirmed</td>
            <td class="text-sm text-mist" data-label="Detail">Added October 19; the older demo description may still call it single-player.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="unknown" class={block}>
    <h2>What is not confirmed</h2>
    <p>
      These are the questions we cannot answer from official material. They are
      not gaps in the game; they are gaps in the public information:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Maximum player count.</strong> No public cap has been announced.</li>
      <li><strong>Dedicated servers.</strong> They have not been mentioned in the store description or launch announcements.</li>
      <li><strong>Cross-play.</strong> No cross-platform details have been published; the confirmed launch platform is Windows/Steam.</li>
      <li><strong>Host migration and save ownership.</strong> No official explanation of what happens if the host leaves or how progression is stored.</li>
      <li><strong>Shared versus separate progression.</strong> The demo is the current reference, not a final rule set.</li>
      <li><strong>Mods and blueprints in multiplayer.</strong> Blueprint sharing is advertised as a general feature, but multiplayer compatibility is not documented.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <span class="callout-title">Use the demo, not a guess</span>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm">
        If you are planning a large group or a stream, test the current demo
        with the exact player count you intend to use. Do not assume the demo’s
        stability or sharing behavior will match the Early Access build.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="start" class={block}>
    <h2>How to start a co-op session</h2>
    <p>
      The exact lobby flow is not documented in the public patch notes, so we
      will not invent a menu path. The reliable starting point is:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Install the current <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/3640620/Main_Sequence_Demo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Main Sequence demo</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open the demo’s multiplayer or co-op menu. The October 19 update is the
        version that added the mode; an older local build may not show it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Decide whether you are using Steam’s online path or a LAN session. Both
        are listed as features on the store page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Before inviting a group, run a short session with one other captain to
        confirm chat, ship access and station permissions in your build.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The demo’s exact player cap and host settings are not public. If the
      interface shows a cap, that is the practical limit for that build, not
      necessarily the final Early Access limit.
    </p>

    <h3>A sensible first-session goal</h3>
    <p>
      Do not start a mega-project on the first co-op night. Complete one shared
      objective: a powered station, one working belt loop or a safe mining run.
      That gives everyone a common point of reference and exposes logistics
      problems early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="crew-planner" class={block}>
    <CrewPlanner />
  </section>

  <section id="roles" class={block}>
    <h2>Role strategy for two to four captains</h2>
    <h3>Two captains: one base, one frontier</h3>
    <p>
      One player owns logistics and production while the other handles defense
      and exploration. This is the cleanest split because it gives the base a
      dedicated operator and keeps the fleet from sitting idle. Rotate after the
      first session so both players learn the other side of the game.
    </p>
    <h3>Three captains: protect the middle</h3>
    <p>
      A dedicated logistics captain, a production captain who also watches
      defense, and an exploration captain works well. The production/defense
      overlap is deliberate: a factory that cannot respond to a threat is a
      factory that will eventually stop.
    </p>
    <h3>Four captains: one primary lane each</h3>
    <p>
      With four players, assign one primary lane per captain: logistics,
      production, defense and exploration. Have each captain write down their
      first objective and what they need from the others. The clean split reduces
      duplicated ships and half-built production lines.
    </p>

    <h3>What every captain should know</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Where the station’s power comes from.</li>
      <li>Which dock or storage area is receiving shared cargo.</li>
      <li>What the current research goal is.</li>
      <li>How to call for help when a combat alert starts.</li>
      <li>Which personal ship is theirs, and where it is parked.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The planner is an editorial aid. It does not set in-game permissions or
      enforce classes. Every crew will need to adapt to its own station layout.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>
